Q: What is HashMap?
❌ “Key value pair” (too shallow)
✅ Correct:
HashMap stores entries in buckets based on hashCode of key.

🔑 What happens when you do:
map.put("A", 10);

Step-by-step:
"A".hashCode() is called
Hash is converted to a bucket index
Bucket is checked:
empty → new Entry added
not empty → collision handling

🔥 MOST IMPORTANT QUESTION:
❓ What if I add a DUPLICATE key?
map.put("A", 10);
map.put("A", 20);

What happens internally?
Hash is calculated again
Bucket is found
HashMap checks:
if (existingKey.equals(newKey))
✅ Key already exists
❌ OLD VALUE REMOVED
✅ NEW VALUE REPLACED
👉 HashMap NEVER stores duplicate keys
Final state:
"A" → 20

🧠 Interview sentence:
“HashMap allows duplicate keys in put calls, but internally replaces the value if key already exists.”

🧪 Why equals() matters (CRITICAL)
class Emp {
    int id;
}
If you don’t override equals() and hashCode():
HashMap will think every object is new
Duplicate logical keys get inserted

➡️ interviews question.
🧠 HashMap Mental Model (MEMORIZE THIS IMAGE)

Bucket
 ├── Entry(key=A, value=10)
 ├── Entry(key=B, value=20)

If same key → value replaced
If different key, same hash → linked list / tree

🧠 Now compare with OTHER MAPS (slowly)
🔹 LinkedHashMap
    Same as HashMap BUT:
    maintains insertion order
    used when order matters (LRU cache)

🔹 TreeMap
    sorted by key
    uses Red-Black Tree
    slower but ordered

🔹 IdentityHashMap (IMPORTANT)
    map.put(new String("A"), 1);
    map.put(new String("A"), 2);

    ❗ IdentityHashMap uses: == instead of equals()
    So it allows logically same keys.

    👉 Used when:
    reference identity matters
    framework internals

🔹 WeakHashMap (VERY IMPORTANT)
    Key idea: Entries disappear automatically when key is no longer referenced.

    Map<Object, String> map = new WeakHashMap<>();
    Object key = new Object();
    map.put(key, "value");
    key = null;

    🧠 After GC: map becomes empty

    Used in:
    caching
    memory-sensitive systems
