Why is HashMap lookup O(1) but List lookup O(n)?
“HashMap lookup is O(1) on average because the key’s hashCode is used to directly compute the bucket index, allowing direct access without traversal.
List lookup is O(n) because elements are stored sequentially and must be traversed one by one to find a value.”

🧠 Mental execution model (VERY IMPORTANT)
HashMap lookup
map.get(key)
What happens:
key.hashCode() → number
Convert hash → bucket index
Jump directly to bucket
Check entry → return value
➡️ No looping over all elements
➡️ That’s why it’s O(1)

List lookup
list.contains(x)

What happens:
Start at index 0
Compare element
Move to index 1
Continue until found or end
➡️ Worst case → check all elements
➡️ That’s O(n)

🔑 Key takeaway (lock this in your mind)
HashMap jumps directly.
List walks step by step.


